<Site title="GEO vs SEO — CiteRank Blog" description="Generative Engine Optimization vs SEO: what actually changes for your content strategy.">
  <section class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Strategy · 7 min read</span>
      <h1>GEO vs SEO: what actually changes for your content</h1>
      <p>Generative Engine Optimization isn’t a rebrand of SEO. It rewards different signals — here’s the honest diff.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <main>
    <article class="post wrap">
      <p><strong>GEO (Generative Engine Optimization)</strong> and <strong>AEO (Answer Engine Optimization)</strong> are two names for the same shift: optimizing for AI systems that generate an answer rather than return a list. The question everyone asks is whether their existing SEO work carries over. The honest answer: <em>some of it does, and some new things matter a lot more.</em></p>

      <h2>What carries over from SEO</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Crawlability</b> — if a bot can’t fetch your page, nothing else matters. Still true, but now there are more bots (GPTBot, PerplexityBot, ClaudeBot) and more ways to block them (WAFs, not just robots.txt).</li>
        <li><b>Authority</b> — brands the model already trusts get cited more. Off-page reputation still compounds.</li>
        <li><b>Structured data</b> — schema was useful for rich results; it’s even more useful as a clean, machine-readable summary for LLMs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What’s genuinely new</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Extractability over ranking</b> — you’re not competing for position #1; you’re competing to be a quotable sentence inside one answer. Lead with the answer, in plain language.</li>
        <li><b>Rendering matters more</b> — Google renders JavaScript; most AI crawlers don’t. A React SPA can rank on Google and be invisible to ChatGPT at the same time.</li>
        <li><b>Share of voice, not rank tracking</b> — the new metric is “in what % of AI answers for my topics do I appear, vs competitors?”</li>
        <li><b>Citations as currency</b> — statistics, named sources and clear entities measurably increase the odds a model cites you (Princeton’s GEO research).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The practical playbook</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Confirm every AI crawler can reach and read your key pages (JS-render check).</li>
        <li>Lead each section with a concise, quotable answer; phrase headings as real questions.</li>
        <li>Add FAQ blocks, comparison tables and schema so content is easy to lift.</li>
        <li>Name your entities and cite your sources with numbers.</li>
        <li>Measure your citation rate in real engines and close the gaps.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>CiteRank is built for exactly this loop — <a href="/">check access</a>, <a href="/audit">audit and fix</a>, then verify in real engines.</p>

